## Welcome to your `fastapi` project!

This agent follows the [fastapi best practices](https://github.com/zhanymkanov/fastapi-best-practices) layout.

Dependency management uses [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/). Common commands:

- `uv sync` — install/refresh the venv from `pyproject.toml` + `uv.lock`
- `uv add <package>` — add a runtime dependency
- `uv add --dev <package>` — add a dev dependency
- `uv run pytest` — run tests
- `uv run uvicorn src.main:app --reload` — run the dev server

`codefly run service <name>` does all of the above for you via the agent.

## Every call your service serves is admitted by the authority's hop

`src/hop.py` installs the authority's verifier on the FastAPI app, ahead of
every router, and on the gRPC server when you have one. There is no switch:
your service refuses to start until the composition delivers `authority/endpoint`
(where the published keys are read), `CODEFLY__MODULE` (the audience the
authority knows your module by) and, outside a local run,
`hop/replay-connection` (the Postgres each single-use admission is reserved in,
shared by every replica).

`/health` and `/ready` are the only paths served unauthenticated, because a
probe is not a caller. Every route you add is admitted like the scaffold's, with
no code of yours: read who the call is for from the verified identity on
`request.state.codefly_identity` — or `src.rpc.server.CALLER` in a gRPC
servicer — never from a header.

`tests/conftest.py` runs the authority and its hop the way your tests need them.
`stamp("GET /your-route")` is the headers of one call the hop let through; the
gRPC equivalent is the full procedure, `stamp("/api.EchoService/Echo")`.
